WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:18.720
The federal minimum wage of $7.25 an hour&nbsp;
has not been raised since 2009. That’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.080
longest period without an increase&nbsp;
since the minimum wage was enacted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:28.240
meaning today’s minimum wage is actually&nbsp;
worth far less than it was in 2009.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.720
This is an insult to American workers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:36.480
and bad for our economy. It’s far past&nbsp;
time to raise the minimum wage to at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:43.840
$15 an hour. Today’s minimum wage is a&nbsp;
starvation wage. A full-time minimum wage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:50.160
worker cannot afford a two-bedroom rental in any&nbsp;
city, county, or state in the entire country.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:56.320
Meanwhile, billionaires like Jeff Bezos can afford&nbsp;
a D.C. mansion with 25 bathrooms and 5 living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:02.400
rooms — just one of his many mansions. Now, the&nbsp;
current minimum wage isn’t so low because these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.000
workers are “worth” less than they were years ago;&nbsp;
quite the opposite. If the minimum wage had kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:14.800
pace with workers’ productivity increases&nbsp;
since 1968, it would be $24 an hour today.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.440
Even so, conservatives are out to&nbsp;
scare you about raising the wage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.640
Let’s debunk each of their talking points.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.600
Myth #1: If businesses have&nbsp;
to raise wages, they’ll cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:34.720
employees’ hours or cut jobs altogether.&nbsp;
Rubbish. Since at least the 1930s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:40.400
critics have argued that setting any minimum&nbsp;
standard of decency at the workplace will raise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:46.480
employer costs and kill jobs — child labor laws,&nbsp;
the 40-hour workweek, and workplace safety laws.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:55.680
Look: Treating workers decently is worth the&nbsp;
price. Besides, an abundance of research shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:01.600
that increases in the minimum wage do not reduce&nbsp;
the overall number of jobs. Researchers examined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:08.000
138 state-level minimum wage increases&nbsp;
and found that the number of low-wage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:14.000
jobs remained essentially unchanged in the five&nbsp;
years following the increase, but paid more.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.120
That’s a job upgrade, not a job loss,&nbsp;
and multiple studies have come to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.840
same conclusion. When I led the fight&nbsp;
to raise the minimum wage in 1996,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:31.200
many conservatives predicted huge job losses.&nbsp;
Well, I’m happy to report that after the increase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:37.520
almost 10 million low-wage workers received a&nbsp;
raise with no decline in overall employment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.720
It’s simply a myth that raising the&nbsp;
wage automatically means lost jobs.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.200
Not to mention the benefits for workers&nbsp;
themselves. Raising the wage to $15&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:55.520
an hour by 2025, as proposed in the Raise the&nbsp;
Wage Act, would give 32 million workers a raise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:00.880
Here’s the bottom line: If your business model&nbsp;
depends on paying your workers starvation wages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.600
you should not be in business.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:10.240
Myth #2: Small businesses won’t be able to afford&nbsp;
the higher wage and will be put out of business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:19.040
Baloney. The fact is, a higher minimum wage can&nbsp;
actually lower costs to small businesses. How?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:25.680
For starters, a higher minimum wage attracts&nbsp;
more potential workers into the labor force,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.960
thereby giving employers more choice of whom&nbsp;
to hire. This leads to higher productivity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:36.080
and better service. Better service means more&nbsp;
satisfied clients and customers. Higher paid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:41.680
workers are also more likely to stick around,&nbsp;
saving businesses the hefty costs that come with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:47.840
recruiting, hiring, and training new workers. A&nbsp;
study of the San Francisco airport confirms this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:54.400
researchers found that following a wage increase,&nbsp;
a majority of workers who received a raise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:59.840
improved their overall performance. The higher&nbsp;
wages even led to shorter airport lines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:07.280
Researchers also found that employee turnover&nbsp;
declined by 34 percent -- saving employers an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:13.600
estimated $6.6 million a year. Smart&nbsp;
business owners understand this. Henry Ford,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:20.240
after introducing the “five dollar day” in 1914&nbsp;
when the typical industry wage was less than half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:25.760
that, called it his best cost-cutting strategy&nbsp;
because of the productivity boost that followed.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:34.000
Myth #3: If the wage is raised, prices for&nbsp;
everything will skyrocket and lead to widespread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:42.320
inflation. Wrong again. Researchers have found&nbsp;
that for every 10 percent increase in the minimum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:48.960
wage, prices increase by less than half a percent.&nbsp;
And it’s a temporary price increase — occurring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:54.720
only in the month the wage hike goes into&nbsp;
effect. No way this sparks inflation.In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.720
the minimum wage needs to be raised so&nbsp;
that it can catch up with inflation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:06.080
Because of inflation, the minimum wage is now&nbsp;
worth almost a third less than it was in 1968.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:13.200
And since it was last raised in 2009, it’s&nbsp;
lost 17 percent of its value. This means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:22.720
compared to 2009, minimum wage workers have lost&nbsp;
$3,950 every year. That’s why a higher minimum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:30.000
wage would boost economic growth. 70 percent&nbsp;
of the economy depends on consumer spending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.200
so more money in people’s pockets means they can&nbsp;
spend more on the goods and services that keep the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:41.280
economy going. Oh, and raising the minimum wage&nbsp;
would reduce the amount of money taxpayers spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:47.120
on public assistance that families need because&nbsp;
their breadwinners don’t make enough to live on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.560
It’s estimated that nearly half of&nbsp;
federal minimum wage workers’ families&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.560 --> 00:06:00.240
are enrolled in at least one safety net program,&nbsp;
costing the public $107 billion every year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:06.400
That’s right: our tax dollars are subsidizing&nbsp;
corporations that don’t pay a living wage.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:13.520
Myth #4: Most minimum wage workers are teenagers&nbsp;
making some extra money on the side; they don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:20.960
need a wage increase. More rubbish. While this&nbsp;
might have been the case in 1968, it certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:29.200
isn't now. Today, only 1 in 10 workers who would&nbsp;
benefit from a $15 minimum wage is a teenager.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:37.760
More than half are between the ages of 25 and 54.&nbsp;
More than half of them work full time, and over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:44.480
a quarter have children. Today’s starvation wage&nbsp;
hurts people who are in their prime earning years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:45.920
preventing them from building wealth and&nbsp;
establishing financial security. building wealth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:51.760
and establishing financial security. Raising&nbsp;
the minimum wage would also help reduce racial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:57.840
and gender pay disparities. Minimum wage&nbsp;
increases and expansions in the late 1960s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:06.560
reduced the income gap between Black and&nbsp;
white workers. because Black workers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:12.960
Hispanic workers, and women comprise a large&nbsp;
portion of today’s low-wage workers. In sum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:20.000
raising the minimum wage is good for workers,&nbsp;
good for businesses, and good for the economy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:25.120
In addition to all this, raising the minimum&nbsp;
wage is the morally correct thing to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:30.560
It ought to lift working people out of poverty,&nbsp;
not keep them in it. We’re the richest country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:37.440
in the world, home to the richest people on the&nbsp;
planet. We can, and we must, treat our workers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:57.840
with the dignity and respect they deserve.&nbsp;
That starts with paying them a living wage.

